From 1c2f2c92e0d9326ad2a55a833165ab6e70e5d746 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zirize <zirize@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 01:40:44 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Mark a page an agent write changed, and ask the 6502
 about it

mem_write has been dropping writes. When the page a write lands on is the one
currently mapped in, the byte goes into the live image rather than into the
bank's own store, and the next soft switch copies a page back to its bank only
if something says the page changed. The CPU says so for its own writes. A
write arriving over the socket had nothing saying so, so the switch threw it
away and copied the bank's older byte in over it.

For a game keeping its data in the language card that is a fraction of a
second. A cheat read back correctly, held for as long as nothing happened, and
then let go with nothing to show that it had.

mem_mark_dirty() says a page changed, and mem_write calls it for a write that
lands on the live image - not for one that lands in a bank's store, where
marking the live page would copy the unchanged page back over the write at the
next switch instead. The test helpers that poke memory do the same, which is
how the aux case below was failing on its setup rather than on its subject.

Five cases, and what they have in common is that none of them checks a value
this file decided on. They plant a program at $0300 and ask the 6502:

  - a byte written through bank 1 reads back through "cpu" and not through
    "main" - and a byte written through "cpu" is still there after the program
    pages the bank out and back, which is the case that was failing;
  - all sixteen of $C080-$C08F, each one selected by the program itself, with
    the cpu view's answer checked against the program's own. The bit patterns
    are not obvious and the table is not repeated here: the machine is asked
    what it ended up with;
  - the aux view, which had never been compared against a running program at
    all, in both directions - the program writes through $C005 and the agent
    reads it, the agent writes and the program reads it back through 80STORE;
  - and the same swapped-out-page trap one bank over, which was failing too.

The sweep also pins down where the two banks live in the main image: bank 2 at
$D000-$DFFF and bank 1 $1000 lower, so "main" at $DA80 is bank 2 and "main" at
$CA80 is bank 1. That is the $CA80 that sat in the notes as if it were an
address of its own.

51 cases.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
